  "summary": "Stencilled rice-paste resist and indigo: Edo patterns printed by knife-cut katagami paper.",
  "description": "Katazome prints cloth by the negative: intricate patterns cut into smoked katagami stencil paper — pine needles, trailing water, dragonfly wings — are laid on cloth, rice paste brushed through, then the whole yard is dyed; washing the paste away leaves the pattern as untouched white. Ise katagami guilds carved stencils fine as lace with cut awls and punches; indigo katazome dressed farmers, decorated futon covers, noren curtains and samurai under-kimono. The stencil vocabulary feeds directly into modern Japanese graphic design.",
